  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 228 AM EST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nThe extended periods begin Tue night with a high pressure wedge \nbuilding down the eastern seaboard. The models show this ridge \ncentered over the New England states keeping things dry through \ndaybreak Wednesday. The pressure gradient looks to be fairly tight \nacross the area as this wedge builds in so could see some breezy \nconditions Tues across the state.  The models seem to be coming into \nbetter agreement with showing the upcoming active weather pattern \ndeveloping from mid to late week. \n\nBy late Tuesday, the pattern starts to shift as the first of two \nshortwaves push east out of TX and across the Gulf Coast. Both the \nGFS and ECMWF continue to have differences on the track, speed, and \nstrength of this initial wave, but they both are showing a fairly \nsimilar pattern emerging with S to SW flow increasing moisture \nacross GA by Wed morning. Rain chances will increase from south to \nnorth throughout the day Wednesday, with widespread rain covering \nthe area by the evening hours. Still not see much in the way of \ninstability so continuing with just showers in the forecast.  In \naddition to the rain, winds will become quite breezy out of the E/NE \nas the pressure gradient strengthens with a low developing to our \nsoutheast. One of the biggest unknowns with the forecast is how long \nthe moisture and forcing linger over Georgia, which model guidance \nhas relatively poor agreement on. The ECMWF shows the shortwave \nquickly moving NE by Thursday morning, but the GFS have the \nshortwave (and nearby surface low) being stronger and slower, with \nbackside moisture/rain lingering through at least Thursday night. \nDecided to go slit the difference between the two, with rain \ntapering off from South to North beginning Thu morning. When the \nprecip ends will play a huge role in precipitation totals but for \nnow going with 0.5-1\" across central GA, and 1-2\" over north GA. A \nslower and stronger system will result in higher rainfall totals and \na potential flood threat. Models do come back into better agreement \non the overall pattern behind this system, with dry and cool air \nmoving in for Friday and Saturday. Stay tuned for updates, \nespecially if you have outdoor plans for the Wed-Fri time period.",
